for as long as I can remember there have  been two people inside my head  whispering to me now I want to introduce  them to you and I think the best way to  do that is with an experience I had last  year in probably my least favorite place  in the entire world the customer service  line at United Airlines  I had gotten to SFO an hour early to  have plenty of time to make my direct  flight to JFK I was looking forward to  landing late that evening and getting a  good night's sleep before my friend's  wedding the following afternoon instead  as I was going through security I got a  text from United saying my flight was  canceled for airplane maintenance I  rushed to the customer service line  where after waiting my turn the woman  behind the counter told me there was no  way I could get to New York before  Sunday my response wait you're telling  me it'll take two days to get from San  Francisco to New York that can't be  right please check again the woman did  not take kindly to my tone and pushed a  few buttons before telling me I could  take a two-layer of a trip and make the  tail end of the reception take it or  leave it  I tried after the fact to be friendly  but couldn't get any other options and I  took it later I found out a friend  behind me in line had patiently managed  to get a one-layer of her trip and make  the ceremony so that's what happened  outside my head out in the world but  here's what happened with those two  people inside my head now the first  person imagine him whispering to me from  my left shoulder I called the eager  updater I got to the airport saw the  cancellation text and the eager updater  chimed right into my head he said hmm  you know it looks like the plane we  expected to take us to New York isn't  going there after all good to know  what's our best option now as I dealt  with the customer service lady the eager  updater immediately noticed she wasn't  the instant Ally I'd been counting on he  said good to know now we can adjust our  approach to get the best outcome from  the woman who is in front  of us and not the woman we thought she'd  be pretty mature right kind of  suspicious though like if the eager  updater was whispering all this useful  stuff to me how come I acted so  ineffectively  well that's where the second person in  my head comes in I call him the king of  wishful thinking imagine him whispering  to me from my right shoulder now the  king of wishful thinking saw the  cancellation text and he too chimed  right into my head he said this  shouldn't be happening  we were just about to be on our way to  New York and now we have to deal with a  frustrating day of travel hassle every  minute I stood in the customer service  line the king of wishful thinking was  whispering reminders to me you know we'd  expected to be in the air by now by now  we'd be over Colorado on our way to a  nice warm hotel room as I dealt with the  customer service lady the king of  wishful thinking said to me if someone  competent was back there we would they  would have fixed this by now this  shouldn't be happening so those are the  two folks whispering in my head all the  time  the eager updater with his various forms  of good to know and the king of wishful  thinking riffing on his awesome  catchphrase this shouldn't be happening  and if these guys are starting to sound  familiar to you they should because  these two characters are whispering in  your head all the time to the eager  updater and the king of wishful thinking  are just two ways our brains are wired  to receive and process information over  the past couple years I've gotten really  interested in the neuroscience and  psychology behind how we handle  situations and I've been starting in  particularly this duality between  perception and expectation I gave names  and personalities to these mental  processes really just to help me more  easily apply what I've learned to begin  to make better decisions in my own life  and I want to share them with you  because I think that getting more  acquainted with these guys in each of  our heads can help us handle be be more  effective in handling a wide variety of  situations we face every day so I think  of the eager updater as  this guy that openly seeks out new  information to update our view of what  is happening in any situation he plugs  into our five senses to add more data so  we can better understand accept and  respond to the real world in which we  operate the king of wishful thinking on  the other hand filters every experience  we have through the lens of our  expectations our hopes our beliefs our  anticipations about what will happen or  what should happen in any situation  now maybe you're having visions right  now of old cartoons with an eager  updater angel on one shoulder and a king  of wishful thinking devil on the other  that's not fair to the king of wishful  thinking we evolved this expectation  lens for very good reasons for one being  able to set a future goal and hold it in  mind keeps us motivated and proactive as  we work to achieve it it also keeps us  mentally efficient allowing us to focus  on just the most relevant parts of our  surroundings the neuroscience research  for example suggests that our eyes  actually use our expectations to fill  out most of what we see with just small  amounts of new data in forming the  picture the problem though is that even  though the king of wishful thinking is  useful and necessary he does have a  tendency to get much louder than our  eager updater and drown him out and  that's what happened with my experience  with United Airlines but this tendency  applies well beyond changing travel  plans so for example an over assertive  king of wishful thinking contributed to  my staying in relationships too long  when I clung to an image of the girl I  hoped I was dating and filtered out the  information in the real world that was  showing me something different or beyond  my own personal experience I was  chatting about this topic last month  with an entrepreneur here in the valley  who immediately recognized the king of  wishful thinking in himself as the  number one reason his first startup  hadn't taken off for one thing he said  he had really wanted all of his early  employees to be high performers and so  didn't acknowledge soon enough that some  of them were not an act  khalida course correct similarly he said  he had expected that the product  offering he'd come up with would be very  difficult to replicate and so wasn't  attentive to the competitors that  started to enter and gain market share  now hopefully each of these experiences  reinforces that our king of wishful  thinking can be allowed and damaging  part of our psychology and our  neurophysiology but that doesn't mean we  just have to throw up our hands the  research suggested are two relatively  simple steps we can take to bring him  more in line and the first step is  simply to recognize that we all have a  king of wishful thinking whispering  inside our heads all the time and be  aware when he might be getting too  dominant  now here the science suggests that there  are five emotions that are the most  useful red flags for this and as I list  them think about a time you've recently  felt these frustration anger jealousy  irritation disappointment recognize  those I know I felt three or four of  them this morning before breakfast well  it turns out that these are signals of  an over aggressive king of wishful  thinking basically telling us that  there's something that we were hoping  for or expecting from reality that isn't  they're basically telling us this  shouldn't be happening the second step  then is in those situations to summon a  more attentive and assertive eager  updater and the main strategy here is to  open out our perceptual field by asking  questions like how might I respond  differently now given the new  information available or by responding  to the Kings this shouldn't be happening  with this is happening good to know with  just these two steps we can change the  way we experience the setbacks and  opportunities in our lives with the  benefit of a more clear sighted and  constructive view of what's going on  around us and that's my hope for myself  and it's my hope for each of you  that next time you're working hard to  solve a complex problem or grow a  promising company or take the next step  in a relationship or heaven forbid  reschedule flight on United I hope you  stop take a mental step back and  readjust the volume settings of the two  companions whispering from your  shoulders
 

